        },
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "J3 Conn_01x03 and J8 Conn_01x03: pin 1 connects to the D2/capacitor rail; pin 2 connects to GND."
        }
      ],
      "impact": "A standard servo plugged into J3/J8 would receive reverse polarity on its power pins, likely preventing operation and potentially damaging the servo or the board's servo power rail.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    },
    {
      "category": "short_to_supply",
      "components": ["J1", "J2"],
      "pins": ["J1.25", "J2.25"],
      "nets": ["+5V"],
      "observed": "The +5V node is tied to J1/J2 pin 25, while J1/J2 pin 27 is labelled PIN27. The specification states +5V should be tied to pin 27 of the M5Stack bus connectors.",
      "expected": "+5V should be connected to J1/J2 pin 27, the M5Stack bus 5V pin, not pin 25.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "The same +5V net is also tied directly to J1/J2 pin 27 (the M5Stack bus's 5V pin)."
        },
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "J1/J2 left-side pin 25 has the global label '+5V'; pin 27 is labelled 'PIN27'."
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If pin 25 is not the M5Stack 5V pin, the external/board 5V rail is connected to the wrong bus pin, potentially applying 5V to a GPIO or other signal pin and damaging the M5Stack or preventing proper 5V distribution.",
      "confidence": 0.65
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic contains at least one clear deviation: J3/J8 route servo power to pin 1 and ground to pin 2, opposite the specification's pin 2 power assignment. A second likely deviation is that +5V is placed on J1/J2 pin 25 instead of the specified pin 27."
}

